   1→# VEX Corpus: Edge & Topology
   2→
   3→> 88 examples from vex-corpus. Sources: cgwiki-vex, joy-of-vex-youtube, sidefx-vex-reference
   4→
   5→## Beginner (2 examples)
   6→
   7→### Linear vertex or find the starting point on every curve â
   8→
   9→```vex
  10→i@a = @vtxnum;
  11→```
  12→
  13→I did this a while ago, forgot, Remi and Legomyrstan and Swalsch and Ulyssesp gave me the answers I needed.
  14→
  15→Say you have lots of curves, and want to identify the first point in each curve.
  16→
  17→### Ensure unique names â
  18→
  19→```vex
  20→@name += '_' + itoa(@primnum);
  21→```
  22→
  23→Good question from lovely friend and coworker Ben Skinner .
  24→
  25→## Intermediate (80 examples)
  26→
  27→### Cross product â
  28→
  29→```vex
  30→@N = cross(@N, {0,1,0});
  31→```
  32→
  33→Switch to the sphere now (or if you're not using my hip, create a sphere in polygon mode, frequency around 5, append a normal sop in point mode).
  34→
  35→### Cross Product Introduction
  36→
  37→```vex
  38→@N = cross(@N, {0,1,0});
  39→```
  40→
  41→Computing the cross product between the point normal and the up vector {0,1,0}, which produces a new vector perpendicular to both input vectors.
  42→
  43→### Cross Product Right Hand Rule
  44→
  45→```vex
  46→vector tmp = cross(@N, {0,1,0});
  47→@N = cross(@N, tmp);
  48→```
  49→
  50→Demonstrates the right-hand rule for cross products by first computing a perpendicular vector from the normal and up vector, then using that temporary vector to compute a second perpendicular direc....
  51→
  52→### Cross product vector orientation
  53→
  54→```vex
  55→@N = cross(@N, {1,1,0});
  56→```
  57→
  58→Uses the cross product to compute a new normal direction perpendicular to both the original normal and the vector {1,1,0}.
  59→
  60→### Cross Product for Normal Calculation
  61→
  62→```vex
  63→@N = cross(@v, {1,1,0});
  64→
  65→vector tmp = cross(@v, {0,1,0});
  66→@N = cross(tmp, @v);
  67→```
  68→
  69→Demonstrates using the cross product to compute normals by crossing velocity vectors with arbitrary axis vectors.
  70→
  71→### Double Cross Product
  72→
  73→```vex
  74→vector tmp = cross(@N, {0, -1, 0});
  75→@N = cross(@N, tmp);
  76→```
  77→
  78→Demonstrates a double cross product operation where the normal is first crossed with a vector, stored in a temporary variable, then crossed again with the original normal.
  79→
  80→### Cross product for hair combing
  81→
  82→```vex
  83→vector tmp = cross(@N, {0, 1, 0});
  84→@N = cross(@N, tmp);
  85→```
  86→
  87→Creates a temporary vector by crossing the point normal with the up vector, then crosses the normal with this temp vector to produce a combed-down effect.
  88→
  89→### Cross Product for Gravity Combing
  90→
  91→```vex
  92→vector tmp = cross(@N, {0, 1, 0});
  93→@N = cross(@v, tmp);
  94→```
  95→
  96→Creates a temporary vector by crossing the normal with the up vector, then recalculates the normal by crossing velocity with that temporary vector.
  97→
  98→### Gravity-Based Vector Combing
  99→
 100→```vex
 101→vector tmp = cross(@N, {0,1,0});
 102→@N = cross(tmp, @N);
 103→```
 104→
 105→Creates a gravity-based combing effect by computing two successive cross products to align normals downward.
 106→
 107→### Double Cross Product for Grooming
 108→
 109→```vex
 110→vector tmp = cross(@N, {0,1,0});
 111→tmp = cross(@N, tmp);
 112→tmp = cross(@N, tmp);
 113→tmp = cross(@N, tmp);
 114→@N = cross(@N, tmp);
 115→```
 116→
 117→Demonstrates a double cross product technique commonly used in grooming operations to orient all normals downward, creating a gravity-based directional flow.
 118→
 119→### Iterative Cross Product Rotations
 120→
 121→```vex
 122→vector cross1 = cross(@N, {0,1,0});
 123→cross1 = cross(@N, cross1);
 124→cross1 = cross(@N, cross1);
 125→cross1 = cross(@N, cross1);
 126→@N = cross(@N, cross1);
 127→```
 128→
 129→Demonstrates iterative application of the cross product to rotate normals around an axis.
 130→
 131→### Sequential Cross Products for Vector Rotation
 132→
 133→```vex
 134→vector cross1 = cross(@N, {0,1,0});
 135→cross1 = cross(@N, cross1);
 136→@N = cross(@N, cross1);
 137→```
 138→
 139→Demonstrates sequential cross product operations to rotate normals around an axis.
 140→
 141→### Iterative Cross Product Application
 142→
 143→```vex
 144→vector cross1 = cross(@N, {0,1,0});
 145→cross1 = cross(@N, cross1);
 146→cross1 = cross(@N, cross1);
 147→cross1 = cross(@N, cross1);
 148→cross1 = cross(@N, cross1);
 149→@N = cross(@N, cross1);
 150→```
 151→
 152→Demonstrates iterative application of the cross product, repeatedly computing perpendicular vectors from the point normal.
